L000153	\begin{document}
L000154	\title{The diffusion equation is compatible with special relativity}
L000155	\author{L.~Gavassino}
L000156	\affiliation{Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, University of Cambridge, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge CB3 0WA, United Kingdom}
L000157	
L000158	
L000159	\begin{abstract}
L000160	Due to its parabolic character, the diffusion equation exhibits instantaneous spatial spreading, and becomes unstable when Lorentz-boosted. According to the conventional interpretation, these features reflect a fundamental incompatibility with special relativity. In this Letter, we show that this interpretation is incorrect by demonstrating that any smooth and sufficiently localized solution of the diffusion equation is the particle density of an exact solution of the relativistic Vlasov-Fokker-Planck equation. This establishes the existence of a causal, stable, and thermodynamically consistent relativistic kinetic theory whose hydrodynamic sector is governed \emph{exactly} by diffusion at all wavelengths. We further demonstrate that the standard arguments for instability arise from considering solutions that admit no counterpart in kinetic theory, and that apparent violations of causality disappear once signals are defined in terms of the underlying microscopic data.
L000161	\end{abstract}
L000162	\maketitle
L000163	
L000164	{\it \noindent \textbf{Introduction --}} Fick's law of diffusion \cite{Fick1855},
L000165	\vspace{-0.25cm}
L000166	\begin{equation}\label{diffusion}
L000167	\partial_t n = \mathfrak{D}\,\partial_x^2 n \, ,
L000168	\end{equation}
L000169	is among the most ubiquitous equations in physics. It describes the evolution of a conserved density \(n(t,x)\) whose microscopic carriers undergo stochastic motion characterized by a diffusivity \(\mathfrak{D}\). As a result, it provides the universal long-wavelength description of transport processes in a wide variety of systems \cite{KadanoffMartin1963}, including particle diffusion in fluids \cite{peliti_book}, heat conduction in both fluids \cite{landau6} and solids \cite{landau7}, and the probability spreading of Brownian motion \cite{Einstein1905Brownian}.
L000170	
L000171	Despite its broad applicability, the diffusion equation is widely regarded in the relativistic hydrodynamics literature as a paradigmatic example of a dissipative theory incompatible with special relativity
L000172	\cite{MorseFeshbach1953,cattaneo1958,Israel_Stewart_1979,Hiscock_Insatibility_first_order,Baier2008,Muller_book,Jou_Extended,rezzolla_book,RomatschkeReview:2017ejr,GavassinoFronntiers2021,GavassinoSuperlum2021,RochaReview:2023ilf}.
L000173	In particular, its retarded Green function, defined as the solution of the inhomogeneous equation
L000174	\(\partial_t G = \mathfrak{D}\partial_x^2 G + \delta(t)\delta(x)\) with \(G(0^-,x)=0\),
L000175	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000176	\begin{equation}
L000177	G(t,x)=\Theta(t)\frac{e^{-x^2/(4\mathfrak{D}t)}}{\sqrt{4\pi \mathfrak{D}t}},
L000178	\end{equation}
L000179	exhibits Gaussian spatial tails extending to infinity at any \(t>0\), suggesting instantaneous propagation.
L000180	Furthermore, upon Lorentz transformation, equation \eqref{diffusion} develops exponentially growing Fourier modes at arbitrarily large wavenumber
L000181	\cite{Hiscock_Insatibility_first_order,Kost2000,GavassinoSuperlum2021},
L000182	rendering the boosted initial-value problem unstable and ill-posed.
L000183	
L000184	
L000185	These undesired features of diffusion have prompted two distinct responses. The prevailing approach has been to interpret them as genuine pathologies, and to eliminate them by modifying Fick’s law, giving rise to a broad class of hyperbolic relativistic dissipative theories \cite{Geroch_Lindblom_1991_causal,GavassinoUniveraalityI2023odx}, including Cattaneo-type models \cite{cattaneo1958,Jou_Extended}, Israel-Stewart theory \cite{Israel_Stewart_1979,Hishcock1983,Baier2008,Denicol2012Boltzmann}, divergence-type formulations \cite{Liu1986,GerochLindblom1990}, and the BDNK framework \cite{Bemfica2019_conformal1,Kovtun2019,BemficaDNDefinitivo2020,GavassinoAntonelli:2025umq}. An alternative viewpoint maintains that the diffusion equation is not intrinsically incompatible with relativity, but instead appears problematic only when extended beyond the class of solutions warranted by its microscopic origin \cite{Kost2000,Geroch1995,LindblomRelaxation1996,GerochCriticism:2001xs}. This perspective has recently been sharpened by proposals advocating ``approximate'' Lorentz transformations of diffusive dynamics, designed to exclude the unphysical branch of solutions \cite{ArmasDensityFrame2020mpr,Basar:2024qxd,GavassinoParabolic2025hwz}.
L000186	
L000187	
L000188	
L000189	In this Letter, we resolve this long-standing tension by demonstrating that the diffusion equation is, indeed, fully compatible with relativity when interpreted within the correct microscopic framework. We construct a fully relativistic, causal, and stable kinetic theory whose hydrodynamic sector is governed \emph{exactly} by the diffusion equation, without introducing additional gradient corrections, wavenumber cutoffs, or a finite propagation speed. This explicit realization shows that, from a physical standpoint, the apparent acausality and instability of diffusion do not reflect a fundamental inconsistency: the commonly cited pathologies arise solely from applying the diffusion equation to non-normalizable configurations that lie outside the solution space permitted by the underlying relativistic dynamics, and relativistic causality (interpreted as the physical impossibility of superluminal messaging) is preserved once we keep track of all the available microscopic information. We thus revisit the standard arguments for acausality and instability and show that they fail once diffusion is treated as a consistent and complete sector of a relativistic kinetic theory.
L000190	
L000191	{\it \noindent \textbf{Notation --}} Througout the article, we work in natural units, $c=\hbar=k_B=1$.
L000192	
L000193	{\it \noindent \textbf{The Vlasov-Fokker-Planck equation --}} Consider a relativistic particle of mass \(m\,{>}\, 0\) confined, for simplicity, to one spatial dimension. Denoting its momentum by \(p\), its energy is \(\varepsilon\,{=}\,\sqrt{m^{2}{+}p^{2}}\), and its velocity is \(v\,{=}\,d\varepsilon/dp\,{=}\,p/\varepsilon\). We assume that the particle is immersed in a thermal medium at inverse temperature \(\beta\), and undergoes random momentum exchanges with it. Under these conditions, the particle experiences Brownian motion in momentum space. Accordingly, an ensemble of such particles is described by a kinetic distribution function \(f(t,x,p)\) satisfying the Vlasov-Fokker-Planck (VFP) equation
L000194	\cite{Debbasch,DunkelHanggi}
L000195	\vspace{-0.25cm}
L000196	\begin{equation}\label{vlasovfokkerplanck}
L000197	(\partial_t+v\,\partial_x)f
L000198	=\frac{1}{\beta^{2}\mathfrak{D}}\,
L000199	\partial_p
L000200	\left(
L000201	\partial_p f
L000202	+\beta v f
L000203	\right),
L000204	\end{equation}
L000205	\newpage\noindent where \((\beta^{2}\mathfrak{D})^{-1}\) denotes the momentum-space diffusivity (which is assumed to be a positive constant). Because the particle velocity obeys \(|v|\leq 1\), the theory is manifestly causal. It is moreover covariantly stable and thermodynamically consistent (see e.g. \cite{GavassinoDistrubingMoving:2026klp}). A quick way to verify this is to notice that the free-energy current
L000206	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000207	\begin{equation}
L000208	\mathcal{F}^\mu = \frac{1}{\beta}\int \frac{dp}{2\pi}
L000209	\begin{bmatrix}
L000210	1\\
L000211	v
L000212	\end{bmatrix}
L000213	f(\beta\varepsilon+\ln f-1)
L000214	\end{equation}
L000215	has nonpositive divergence:
L000216	\vspace{-0.15cm}
L000217	\begin{equation}
L000218	\partial_\mu \mathcal{F}^\mu
L000219	= -\frac{1}{\beta^{3}\mathfrak{D}}
L000220	\int \frac{dp}{2\pi f}\,
L000221	\left(
L000222	\partial_p f+\beta v f
L000223	\right)^{2}
L000224	\leq 0\, ,
L000225	\end{equation}
L000226	in agreement with the second law of thermodynamics \cite{Groot1980RelativisticKT,huang_book,cercignani_book}.
L000227	This, combined with the convexity of \(\mathcal{F}^0\), immediately establishes Lyapunov stability of the equilibrium state in any inertial frame \cite{GavassinoGibbs2021,GavassinoCausality2021}.
L000228	
L000229	
L000230	{\it \noindent \textbf{Main Theorem --}} We can now state our main result, which we express in the form of a theorem.
L000231	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000232	\begin{theorem}\label{theo1}
L000233	Let \(g(k)\) be a Schwartz function satisfying \(g^{*}(k)=g(-k)\), and define (for $t\geq 0$)
L000234	\begin{equation}\label{magicsolutions}
L000235	f(t,x,p)
L000236	=
L000237	e^{\alpha-\beta\varepsilon}
L000238	+
L000239	\int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{dk}{2\pi}\,
L000240	g(k)\,
L000241	e^{-\beta\varepsilon - i\beta \mathfrak{D} k p + i k x - \mathfrak{D} k^{2} t},
L000242	\end{equation}
L000243	with \(\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\).
L000244	Then, the following statements hold:
L000245	\begin{itemize}
L000246	  \setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
L000247	  \setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
L000248	  \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
L000249	\item[\textup{\bf(a)}]
L000250	\(f(t,x,p)\) is a smooth, real-valued function, well defined for all \(t\ge0\), and with finite energy-momentum moments.
L000251	
L000252	\item[\textup{\bf(b)}]
L000253	\(f(t,x,p)\) is nonnegative for all \(t\ge0\), provided that
L000254	\(
L000255	\|g\|_{L^{1}}\le 2\pi e^{\alpha}.
L000256	\)
L000257	
L000258	\item[\textup{\bf(c)}]
L000259	\(f(t,x,p)\) is an exact solution of the relativistic VFP equation~\eqref{vlasovfokkerplanck}.
L000260	
L000261	\item[\textup{\bf(d)}]
L000262	The associated particle density
L000263	\(
L000264	n(t,x)=\int \frac{dp}{2\pi}\,f(t,x,p)
L000265	\)
L000266	satisfies the diffusion equation~\eqref{diffusion}, with initial condition
L000267	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000268	\begin{equation}\label{densitykernelrelation}
L000269	n(0,x)
L000270	=
L000271	\frac{e^{\alpha} m K_{1}(m\beta)}{\pi}
L000272	+
L000273	\int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{dk}{2\pi}\,
L000274	g(k)\,
L000275	\frac{m\,K_{1}\!\left(m\beta\sqrt{1+\mathfrak{D}^{2}k^{2}}\right)}
L000276	{\pi\sqrt{1+\mathfrak{D}^{2}k^{2}}}
L000277	\,e^{ikx}.
L000278	\end{equation}
L000279	\end{itemize}
L000280	\end{theorem}
L000281	
L000282	\begin{proof}
L000283	\textup{\bf(a)}
L000284	For \(t\ge0\), the integrand in equation \eqref{magicsolutions} is a Schwartz function of \(k\), ensuring absolute convergence of the integral. Differentiation with respect to \(t\), \(x\), or \(p\) introduces at most polynomial factors in \(k\), which preserve convergence and smoothness. Reality of \(f\) follows directly from the condition \(g^{*}(k)=g(-k)\). The finiteness of all moments $\int \frac{dp}{2\pi} \varepsilon^a p^b f(t,x,p)$ (with $a,b\geq 0$) follows from the fact that $g(k)e^{-\beta \varepsilon}$ is a Schwartz function of $(k,p)$.
L000285	
L000286	\noindent\textup{\bf(b)}
L000287	Using the triangle inequality, one finds
L000288	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000289	\begin{equation}
L000290	\left|
L000291	\int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{dk}{2\pi}\,
L000292	g(k)\,
L000293	e^{-\beta\varepsilon - i\beta \mathfrak{D} k p + i k x - \mathfrak{D} k^{2} t}
L000294	\right|
L000295	\le
L000296	\frac{e^{-\beta\varepsilon}}{2\pi}\,\|g\|_{L^{1}} .
L000297	\end{equation}
L000298	The stated bound on \(\|g\|_{L^{1}}\) therefore guarantees \(f(t,x,p)\ge0\).
L000299	
L000300	\noindent\textup{\bf(c,d)} The last two statements follow by direct substitution. All derivatives may be interchanged with the \(k\)-integral, and the integrand is a Schwartz function also in \(p\), allowing the exchange of \(p\)- and \(k\)-integrations.
L000301	\end{proof}
L000302	
L000303	The theorem establishes that any sufficiently regular solution of the diffusion
L000304	equation \(n(t,x)=n_{0}+\delta n(t,x)\) (defined for \(t\ge0\)), with \(n_{0}\) a constant background and \(\delta n\) a localized perturbation, admits an
L000305	\emph{exact} embedding into a corresponding VFP solution \(f(t,x,p)\) of the form~\eqref{magicsolutions}.
L000306	This embedding is realized by choosing
L000307	\begin{equation}\label{geometro}
L000308	g(k)=\delta n(k)\,
L000309	\frac{\pi\sqrt{1+\mathfrak{D}^{2}k^{2}}}
L000310	{m\,K_{1}\!\left(m\beta\sqrt{1+\mathfrak{D}^{2}k^{2}}\right)}\, ,
L000311	\end{equation}
L000312	where \(\delta n(k)\) denotes the Fourier transform of the initial data
L000313	\(\delta n(0,x)\). Provided that \(g(k)\) is sufficiently regular and the
L000314	perturbation \(\delta n\) sufficiently small, the resulting distribution
L000315	\(f(t,x,p)\) defines a physically admissible kinetic solution whose associated
L000316	particle density coincides identically with \(n(t,x)\). In this
L000317	sense, Fickian diffusion is an exact hydrodynamic sector of the
L000318	relativistic kinetic theory, rather than an approximation or a long-wavelength
L000319	limit.
L000320	
L000321	
L000322	We now clarify which initial data admit a well-defined embedding. If the initial perturbation
L000323	\(\delta n(0,x)\) belongs to the Schwartz class, then its Fourier transform \(\delta n(k)\) is also
L000324	Schwartz, implying that the corresponding \(g(k)\) is smooth. However, \(g(k)\) may not itself be
L000325	Schwartz, since the embedding kernel in \eqref{geometro} grows exponentially as
L000326	\(e^{m\beta|\mathfrak{D}k|}\) at large \(|k|\). Nevertheless, for any strictly positive time \(t>0\),
L000327	the integral representation~\eqref{magicsolutions} acquires the Gaussian factor
L000328	\(e^{-\mathfrak{D}k^{2}t}\), which dominates this exponential growth and ensures convergence. Thus,
L000329	the embedding is unambiguous for all \(t\,{>}\,0\) whenever \(\delta n(0,x)\) is Schwartz (while the
L000330	instant \(t\,{=}\,0\) may require interpretation in a distributional sense). A sufficient condition for the embedding to define a bona fide kinetic distribution already at
L000331	\(t\,{=}\,0\) is that
L000332	\(
L000333	\delta n(k)\,e^{(m\beta\mathfrak{D}+a) |k|}\in L^{1}(\mathbb{R})
L000334	\) (for some arbitrary $a>0$),
L000335	for instance if \(\delta n(0,x)\) extends analytically to the strip
L000336	\(|\mathfrak{Im}\, x| < m\beta \mathfrak{D}+a\).
L000337	
L000338	
L000339	
L000340	
L000341	Let us finally note that, although Theorem~\ref{theo1} assumes $g(k)$ to be Schwartz for simplicity, the
L000342	construction only relies on convergence of the $k$ integral for $t>0$, and therefore
L000343	extends to substantially less regular data, suggesting that a broader class of
L000344	initial profiles $\delta n(0,x)$ (e.g. in $L^p$ or $H^s$) might admit an embedding, in a distributional sense.
L000345	
L000346	\newpage
L000347	
L000348	{\it \noindent \textbf{Why stability is preserved --}}
L000349	The standard argument for the instability of the diffusion equation proceeds as follows \cite{Kost2000,GavassinoLyapunov_2020,GavassinoSuperlum2021}. One considers a density perturbation of the form
L000350	\(\delta n = e^{\Gamma \tilde t}\), where \(\tilde t=\gamma(t-Vx)\) is the time coordinate of an inertial observer moving with velocity \(V\) relative to the medium, and \(\gamma=(1-V^{2})^{-1/2}\). Substituting this ansatz into \eqref{diffusion} yields \(\Gamma=(\mathfrak{D}\gamma V^{2})^{-1}>0\), suggesting that a boosted observer would detect exponential growth of spatially homogeneous perturbations (signaling an instability \cite{Hiscock_Insatibility_first_order}).
L000351	
L000352	We now show that such pathological solutions do not admit an embedding into relativistic kinetic theory. Indeed, configurations proportional to \(e^{\Gamma\gamma(t-Vx)}\) are not spatially localized and therefore fall outside the class of solutions covered by our theorem. More strongly, one can show that any solution of the relativistic VFP equation of the form
L000353	\(
L000354	f(t,x,p)=e^{\alpha-\beta\varepsilon}+\delta f(p)\,e^{\Gamma\gamma(t-Vx)}
L000355	\)
L000356	necessarily satisfies \(\Gamma\le0\), and is thus stable. To see this, note that for this spacetime dependence,  equation \eqref{vlasovfokkerplanck} reduces to
L000357	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000358	\begin{equation}
L000359	\Gamma(1-vV)\,\delta f
L000360	=
L000361	\frac{1}{\beta^{2}\mathfrak{D}\gamma}\,
L000362	\partial_p
L000363	\bigl(
L000364	\partial_p\delta f+\beta v\,\delta f
L000365	\bigr).
L000366	\end{equation}
L000367	Multiplying both sides by \(e^{\beta\varepsilon}(\delta f)^{*}\), integrating over momentum, and solving for \(\Gamma\), one finds
L000368	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000369	\begin{equation}
L000370	\Gamma
L000371	=
L000372	-\frac{\displaystyle
L000373	\int \frac{dp}{2\pi}\,e^{\beta\varepsilon}
L000374	\bigl|
L000375	\partial_p\delta f+\beta v\,\delta f
L000376	\bigr|^{2}}
L000377	{\displaystyle
L000378	\beta^{2}\mathfrak{D}\gamma
L000379	\int \frac{dp}{2\pi}(1-vV)\,e^{\beta\varepsilon}|\delta f|^{2}}
L000380	\le0,
L000381	\end{equation}
L000382	where we used the fact that \(1-vV\geq 1-|V|>0\), and we assumed that $\delta f$ decays to 0 at large $p$.
L000383	
L000384	In summary, the unstable modes of the diffusion equation lie outside the space of solutions that can be embedded into a relativistic kinetic theory. Their existence therefore does not signal a failure of diffusion as a relativistic model. Rather, it reflects the necessity (peculiar to relativity) of restricting Fick’s law to spatially localized initial data. Within this physically admissible solution space, diffusion is again stable \cite{GavassinoLorentzBoostedDiffusion:2026fff}.\footnote{\label{footonafootona} In a companion paper \cite{GavassinoFokkerQuasi:2026zsz}, we compute the full quasi-normal-mode spectrum of the VFP equation. We find that in both the Newtonian and ultrarelativistic regimes the hydrodynamic mode has the diffusive form \(\omega=-i\mathfrak{D}k^{2}\), with no extra corrections. However, while in the Newtonian case this mode exists for all \(k\in\mathbb{C}\), in the ultrarelativistic case it is confined to the strip \(|\mathfrak{Im}\,k|<(2\mathfrak{D})^{-1}\), ensuring that the covariant stability bound \(\mathfrak{Im}\,\omega\le|\mathfrak{Im}\,k|\) \cite{HellerBounds2022ejw,GavassinoBounds2023myj} is respected.}
L000385	
L000386	
L000387	{\it \noindent \textbf{Why microscopic causality is preserved --}}
L000388	The claim that the diffusion equation is acausal stems from the
L000389	observation that an initially compactly supported density perturbation
L000390	\(\delta n(t,x)\) develops unbounded spatial support for \(t>0\), thereby suggesting that the propagation is instantaneous \cite{MorseFeshbach1953,rauch2012partial}. We now show that, when such solutions are
L000391	embedded into the relativistic VFP framework, their
L000392	evolution is, in fact, perfectly causal, from a physical standpoint.
L000393	
L000394	All perturbations of the form~\eqref{magicsolutions} can be written as
L000395	\(\delta f(t,x,p)=e^{-\beta\varepsilon}\,F(t,x-\beta\mathfrak{D}p)\).
L000396	It follows that, if there exists a phase-space point \((x_{0},p_{0})\) such that
L000397	\(\delta f(0,x_{0},p_{0})\propto F(0,x_{0}-\beta\mathfrak{D}p_{0})\neq0\),
L000398	then for any other spatial location \(x_{1}\) one can find a momentum
L000399	\(p_{1}=p_{0}+(x_{1}-x_{0})/(\beta\mathfrak{D})\) for which
L000400	\(\delta f(0,x_{1},p_{1})\propto F(0,x_{0}-\beta\mathfrak{D}p_{0})\neq0\).
L000401	As a consequence, no kinetic distribution of the form~\eqref{magicsolutions}
L000402	can possess compact spatial support at the initial time\footnote{This implies that commonly assumed preparation protocols (such as generating a spatially localized perturbation on top of an otherwise equilibrium microscopic state) do not, in general, lie within the diffusive sector of the relativistic kinetic theory considered here.}. This observation clarifies the origin of the apparent superluminal behavior.
L000403	While the macroscopic density perturbation \(\delta n(t,x)\) may exhibit
L000404	instantaneous spatial tails, the initial phase-space perturbation
L000405	\(\delta f(0,x,p)\) is already nonzero outside the support of \(\delta n(0,x)\).
L000406	No information is therefore transmitted faster than light. The nonlocal tails
L000407	of \(\delta n\) arise from a redistribution between positive and negative
L000408	contributions of \(\delta f\), fully determined by the initial microscopic data
L000409	within the past light cone (see \cite{GavassinoDisperisons2023mad} for a similar argument).
L000410	
L000411	In summary, diffusion appears acausal only if ``signals'' are defined in terms of the macroscopic density \(\delta n\) alone. Once the complete microscopic
L000412	information encoded in the kinetic distribution \(\delta f\) is taken into
L000413	account, the evolution is manifestly causal, and the apparent superluminal
L000414	tails of \(\delta n\) convey no new information to an observer far away.
L000415	
L000416	
L000417	
L000418	{\it \noindent \textbf{The massless limit --}} It is instructive to consider a case in which the kinetic distribution can be
L000419	obtained explicitly. In the ultrarelativistic limit \(m\rightarrow 0\), the embedding
L000420	kernel in equation \eqref{geometro} reduces to the polynomial
L000421	\(\pi\beta(1+\mathfrak{D}^{2}k^{2})\), allowing the integral
L000422	representation~\eqref{magicsolutions} to be evaluated in closed form:
L000423	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000424	\begin{equation}\label{fmassles}
L000425	f(t,x,p)
L000426	=
L000427	e^{\alpha-\beta|p|}
L000428	+\pi \beta\, e^{-\beta|p|}
L000429	\bigl(1-\mathfrak{D}^{2}\partial_{x}^{2}\bigr)\,
L000430	\delta n\!\left(t,x-\beta\mathfrak{D}p\right).
L000431	\end{equation}
L000432	One readily verifies that, for any solution \(\delta n(t,x)\) of the diffusion
L000433	equation (not necessarily localized), the distribution function
L000434	\eqref{fmassles} indeed satisfies the ultrarelativistic VFP equation. By contrast,
L000435	showing that the associated particle density
L000436	\(
L000437	n(t,x)=\int \frac{dp}{2\pi}\,f(t,x,p)
L000438	\)
L000439	coincides with \(n_{0}+\delta n(t,x)\) requires an integration by parts in
L000440	momentum space:
L000441	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000442	\begin{equation}\label{proofofDensity}
L000443	\begin{split}
L000444	n(t,x)
L000445	={}&
L000446	n_{0}
L000447	+\int \frac{dp}{2}\,\beta\,e^{-\beta|p|}
L000448	\bigl(1-\beta^{-2}\partial_{p}^{2}\bigr)\,
L000449	\delta n\!\left(t,x-\beta\mathfrak{D}p\right)\\
L000450	={}&
L000451	n_{0}
L000452	+\int \frac{dp}{2}\,\beta\,
L000453	\bigl(1-\beta^{-2}\partial_{p}^{2}\bigr)e^{-\beta|p|}
L000454	\,\delta n\!\left(t,x-\beta\mathfrak{D}p\right)
L000455	=n_{0}
L000456	+\int dp\,\delta(p)
L000457	\,\delta n\!\left(t,x-\beta\mathfrak{D}p\right)
L000458	=
L000459	n_{0}+\delta n(t,x).
L000460	\end{split}
L000461	\end{equation}
L000462	\newpage\noindent This step is justified only within the subset of diffusive solutions for which
L000463	\(\delta n\) does not grow too rapidly at spatial infinity. As a counterexample,
L000464	consider the unstable modes \(\delta n=e^{(t-Vx)/(\mathfrak{D}V^{2})}\) discussed
L000465	earlier. In this case, the integrand in \eqref{proofofDensity} behaves as
L000466	\(e^{-\beta|p|+\beta p/V}\), which diverges exponentially in one momentum
L000467	direction, resulting in an infinite particle density. These unstable modes
L000468	therefore lie outside the admissible solution space selected by the kinetic
L000469	theory.
L000470	
L000471	The explicit form~\eqref{fmassles} also clarifies the mechanism by which causality
L000472	is preserved. Suppose that the initial density perturbation \(\delta n(0,x)\)
L000473	is supported within the interval \(-R\le x\le R\). For a kinetic distribution
L000474	of the form~\eqref{fmassles}, an observer located far away, at \(x=L\gg R\), can
L000475	nevertheless register a weak precursor in the momentum distribution at his/her location, appearing
L000476	as a localized ``bump'' confined to the range
L000477	\((L-R)/(\beta\mathfrak{D})\le p\le (L+R)/(\beta\mathfrak{D})\). This
L000478	momentum-space feature already encodes the complete spatial profile of
L000479	\(\delta n\), since the operator \(1-\mathfrak{D}^{2}\partial_{x}^{2}\) is
L000480	invertible. It follows that no new information is conveyed when the apparently
L000481	superluminal density signal subsequently reaches the observer: all relevant
L000482	information was already contained in the initial kinetic data.
L000483	
L000484	{\it\noindent\textbf{Generalization to higher dimensions --}}
L000485	We emphasize that our restriction to one spatial dimension was adopted solely for
L000486	convenience.
L000487	The construction extends straightforwardly to $d$ spatial dimensions upon replacing
L000488	$kx$ and $kp$ by the inner products $\mathbf k\!\cdot\!\mathbf x$ and
L000489	$\mathbf k\!\cdot\!\mathbf p$, and promoting the operator
L000490	$\partial_p(\partial_p+\beta v)$ to $\nabla_{\mathbf p}\!\cdot(\nabla_{\mathbf p}+\beta\mathbf v)$.
L000491	With these substitutions, the proof of Theorem~\ref{theo1} is unchanged.
L000492	The only modification concerns the explicit form of the density kernel appearing in
L000493	equations \eqref{densitykernelrelation} and \eqref{geometro}, which acquires a
L000494	dimension-dependent structure through the corresponding $d$-dimensional momentum
L000495	integral.
