L000497	
L000498	{\it \noindent \textbf{Physical interpretation and broader consequences --}} The fact that the solutions \eqref{magicsolutions} solve the diffusion equation at arbitrarily large $k$ does \textit{not} imply that diffusion is the dominant dissipation mechanism of VFP in all regimes. In fact, when gradients are large, the states \eqref{magicsolutions} are not generic, as they are explicitly fine-tuned to remove all non-hydrodynamic contributions, which are shown in a companion paper to have ballistic character \cite{GavassinoFokkerQuasi:2026zsz}. Only in the hydrodynamic regime (i.e. when gradients are small compared to $1/\mathfrak{D}$) are generic solutions well approximated by \eqref{magicsolutions}, and diffusion becomes the dominant sector (and thus the appropriate effective description) of the system.
L000499	
L000500	The main point of this work, however, is not that diffusion is the only relevant transport process in relativistic VFP, but that the diffusive mode $\omega = -i\mathfrak{D}k^2$ \textit{exists as an exact excitation} even at short wavelengths. In all other known relativistic theories, this mode ceases to exist beyond some critical value of $k \in \mathbb{R}$, either because it collides with other excitations \cite{Bajec:2024jez,Brants2025SavingCausality} or because its character changes qualitatively due to higher-order corrections. Indeed, it was conjectured in \cite{Krotscheck1978} that $\omega/k$ always remains finite at large $k$ in relativistic causal theories, a claim that has had repercussions across the relativistic hydrodynamics literature \cite{Pu2010,RomatschkeReview:2017ejr,Kovtun2019}. More recently, it was argued \cite{HellerBounds2022ejw,HellerHydrohedron2023jtd} that the dispersion relation $\omega = -i\mathfrak{D}k^2$ cannot arise from causal microscopic theories, based on the assumption that, if it exists for all real $k$, it should also exist (by analytic continuation) at complex $k$, leading to instabilities under Lorentz boosts. In VFP, however, the diffusive mode ceases to exist as a genuine excitation before entering unstable regions of the complex $k$-plane (see footnote \ref{footonafootona}). Since even a single consistent counterexample suffices to invalidate a general prohibition, diffusion is not intrinsically incompatible with relativity. It can arise as a (possibility exact) sector of a microscopic theory, within which all pathological solutions are physically forbidden.
L000501	
L000502	{\it \noindent \textbf{Discussion and Conclusions --}} We provided a rigorous counterexample to the widely held belief that the diffusion
L000503	equation (and, more generally, parabolic theories of dissipation) cannot consistently arise in a fully relativistic context. We have
L000504	demonstrated that a fully relativistic, causal, and stable microscopic theory\footnote{It is worth emphasizing that, while the Vlasov-Fokker-Planck equation is a perfectly acceptable relativistic kinetic theory when regarded as a stand-alone equation, it is also a strictly Markovian dynamical model. It has been suggested that such exact Markovianity may itself be an idealization in a relativistic
L000505	setting, in the sense that deriving this equation from an underlying Langevin description
L000506	can require a degree of temporal or spatial non-locality
L000507	\cite{Petrosyan:2021lqi,Zaccone2023}. On the other hand, the Langevin equation itself is subject to intrinsic limitations in
L000508	relativistic contexts. Since a relativistic Hamiltonian description of interacting classical
L000509	particles cannot exist \cite{CurrieJordanSudarshan1963NoInteraction,CannonJordan1969NoInteraction}, locality arguments based on such equations are not fundamental, and may be intrinsically unreliable.
L000510	}
L000511	can give rise to the diffusion equation~\eqref{diffusion} as an \emph{exact}
L000512	hydrodynamic sector, without the introduction of ultraviolet cutoffs,
L000513	higher-order gradient corrections, or finite relaxation times of the Cattaneo
L000514	type. The standard claims of instability are revealed to be
L000515	artifacts of extending diffusion beyond its physically admissible solution
L000516	space. When the analysis is restricted to smooth, spatially localized configurations in the rest frame of the medium, no instabilities arise, and the principle of causality (i.e. the impossibility of superluminal information transfer) is preserved once the full microscopic information carried by the kinetic
L000517	distribution is taken into account\footnote{Of course, the diffusion equation still defines an acausal (in the sense of \citet[\S~10.1]{Wald}) initial-value problem when regarded as a stand-alone partial differential equation. In this sense, diffusion is still parabolic. Nevertheless, it becomes compatible with the broader principle of relativistic causality once it is embedded into a larger microscopic theory with additional degrees of freedom, which redefine the notion of a physical signal. A closely analogous situation arises for the relativistic Schr\"odinger equation
L000518	\(
L000519	i\partial_t \psi=\sqrt{m^2-\partial_x^2}\,\psi,
L000520	\)
L000521	which by itself does not define a causal initial-value problem, yet corresponds to the positive-frequency sector of the Klein--Gordon equation
L000522	\(
L000523	-\partial_t^2 \psi=(m^2-\partial_x^2)\psi,
L000524	\)
L000525	which is a perfectly causal relativistic field theory \cite{GavassinoDisperisons2023mad}.}. All results presented here are derived within a linear model with constant coefficients; extensions to nonlinear dynamics are not addressed in this work.
L000526	
L000527	The above results prompt a reassessment of the role played by relativity in
L000528	dissipative hydrodynamics. Our findings do not undermine the extensive body of
L000529	work devoted to constructing hyperbolic relativistic theories of dissipation,
L000530	such as Cattaneo, Israel-Stewart, BDNK, or divergence-type formulations \cite{Geroch_Lindblom_1991_causal,GavassinoUniveraalityI2023odx,cattaneo1958,Israel_Stewart_1979,Hishcock1983,Baier2008,Denicol2012Boltzmann,Liu1986,GerochLindblom1990,Bemfica2019_conformal1,Kovtun2019,BemficaDNDefinitivo2020,GavassinoAntonelli:2025umq}. In
L000531	practice, when one considers boosted frames, rotating fluids, or systems
L000532	without a globally comoving frame, the exact kinetic description leads to
L000533	effective equations that are highly nonlocal and difficult to handle
L000534	numerically. In such situations, hyperbolic hydrodynamic models remain
L000535	indispensable as controlled approximations that ensure manifest Lorentz
L000536	covariance and well-posedness at the level of partial differential equations.
L000537	Moreover, for many physical systems, Israel-Stewart-type theories provide a
L000538	quantitatively superior description of transient dynamics beyond the diffusive
L000539	regime \cite{Jou_Extended,Denicol_Relaxation_2011,Grozdanov2019,BAGGIOLI20201,BulkGavassino,GavassinoFarFromBulk:2023xkt,AhnBaggioli:2025odk,GavassinoPlasma:2025tul}.
L000540	
L000541	At the same time, our analysis challenges a deeply rooted intuition: that
L000542	relativistic effects must necessarily manifest themselves at the hydrodynamic
L000543	level through a finite signal speed \cite{cattaneo1958,Israel_Stewart_1979}. The present results show that this
L000544	expectation is too restrictive. Relativity constrains the microscopic dynamics
L000545	and the admissible space of states, but it does not require the emergent
L000546	hydrodynamic equations themselves to be hyperbolic. In this sense, Cattaneo’s equation is not intrinsically more ``relativistic'' than Fick’s law; it is just more robust as a standalone macroscopic theory, remaining well-behaved over a broader class of states and frames. In other words, the true imprint of relativity lies
L000547	not in the form of the macroscopic equations alone, but in the structure of the
L000548	microscopic theory and in the restrictions it imposes on physically realizable
L000549	hydrodynamic solutions.
L000550	
L000551	More broadly, our work highlights that apparent violations of causality or
L000552	stability in effective theories often signal a mismatch between the equation
L000553	and its domain of applicability, rather than a fundamental inconsistency. By
L000554	identifying diffusion as an exact, relativistically admissible sector of
L000555	kinetic theory, we provide a concrete example in which parabolic dynamics
L000556	coexist fully consistently with relativistic causality. We expect that similar
L000557	considerations may apply to other dissipative processes, and that a careful
L000558	separation between equations of motion and admissible solution spaces will
L000559	prove essential in future developments of relativistic nonequilibrium physics.
